  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pethia-bandula-2/",
      "html": "<p>Pethia bandula is one of a small group of very similar barb species from southern India and Sri Lanka, together with the black ruby barb Pethia nigrofasciata and Pethia narayani. It is even rarer in the aquarium than its relatives and is threatened with extinction in the wild.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pethia-bandula-2/",
      "html": "<p>European-bred Bandula barbs are available again. Young fish only hint at the beauty of adults. It would be desirable if more of these fish were kept, to ensure the survival of the species at least in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pethia-bandula-2/",
      "html": "<p>Sri Lanka. In nature the species inhabits an area of only 4 km².</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pethia-bandula-2/",
      "html": "<p>The main threat was long the accumulation of pesticides from the rice fields in the area where the species occurs; drought events due to climate change are now added to this. It is feared that the species will become completely extinct in nature.</p>"
    }
  ],
